  [ocean-net-haul] grader rejected: The haul reads as four evenly spaced broadband noise bursts with sharp attacks and decays. That sounds like rhythmic pulses or a chugging machine, not a continuous net dragging over a gunwale. A real haul has overlapping rope/mesh friction, irregular pulls and creaks. | The drips are barely visible. At default they show only as faint scattered specks in the tails, so the 'dripping' in the brief is not convincingly delivered. They are not distinct water plinks with a clear pitched, bright character. | The catch flop is not identifiable. No distinct wet, low-mid thump or slap cluster stands out from the pulses, so that knob does not read as a fish. | The four pulses sit at nearly identical, regular intervals and have similar amplitude in every render, which sounds mechanical and loop-like. Seeds change fine detail but not the rhythm, so the two takes feel like the same pattern. | The net choice (nylon vs rope) differs mostly in centroid and the pulses look similar in shape. The difference is subtle and reads as an EQ change, not different materials. | At max range the pulses merge into a dense wash with a low-frequency smear and an unclear tail. At min range the pulses are isolated blocks with gaps. These extremes feel like different textures, not one sound at two intensities. | With the tail off, the final pulse is cut short, with 18% silence between the pulses. This leaves a choppy, gated effect.
ok   ocean-harbour/ocean-crate-set-down published (66s) [15/29]
  [ocean-net-haul] grader rejected: The render is a dense, broadband noise bed with a swelling envelope, which reads as generic rustle or surf rather than a net hauled over a gunwale. Rope drag, mesh scrape and the gunwale contact are not distinct. | Drips should show up as discrete bright transients. In the spectrogram they are faint, scattered ticks buried in the noise, so the dripping character the brief names is barely audible. | The catch flop is not identifiable. No distinct low-mid thump or splashy event appears at any point, so that knob does not read as a separate event. | The minimum-range render is a smooth, symmetric lump with a very bright centroid (3451 Hz). It sounds like a hiss swell and has lost the hauling and dripping identity. The maximum render is better but still mostly noise. | The nylon and rope choices differ mainly in centroid, with a similar texture and envelope. They read as an EQ change rather than two materials. | The seeds share a broad swell shape, but seed 2 is noticeably quieter and flatter (rms 0.132 against 0.168). Event placement is fairly arbitrary, with no rhythmic hauling pattern, so the takes feel like random noise rather than pulls. | The tail toggle only shortens the sound and removes the dripping afterwards. The remaining drips are not clearly distinguishable.
  [ocean-hull-bump-fender] grader rejected: Defaults read as a thud with a thin decaying tail, but the energy is almost all below ~500 Hz with a faint squeak line. It sounds like a generic low thump rather than a hull hitting a fender; there is no clear contact transient, no creak or rub, and no water slosh or hull body. | The fender choices (tyre, foam, rope-bumper) look nearly the same: the same short thud envelope and the same low-frequency band, differing mostly in centroid (377-600 Hz). A designer would not hear three materials, so the main knob adds little. | The range extremes differ in character. The minimum is a barely visible blip with a very short decay. The maximum is a long, ringing, stable-pitch resonance, visible as horizontal lines that last nearly the whole 1.4 s. It reads as a tonal drone or bell and not as a bump, and it is far less natural than the default. | The tail toggle has little effect (centroid 451 to 437 Hz, silence 53% to 61%). The tail is just a quieter faint wash, so this knob is not worth having. | Roughly 50% of each 1.40 s file is silence, with the fixed length padded out after the sound dies. That wastes space and makes the sound feel unfinished as an asset. | The squeak is a few faint short lines in the spectrogram. It is not convincing as rubber on a hull and is easy to miss.
  [ocean-hull-bump-fender] grader rejected: Every render, including the max-range one, is a short thump under about 0.3 s followed by a near-silent flat line, so about 50% of the 1.40 s file is dead air. A hull bump on a fender should have a decaying thud and some rubbery or woody resonant ring, not a single click followed by silence. | The 'tail = false' toggle barely changes anything. The waveform stays similar and silence only rises from 52% to 61%. The tail has no clear audible role, so the knob feels redundant. | The three fender types look almost identical: the same thump, the same low band, and centroids of 377, 451 and 600 Hz. Tyre, foam and rope-bumper do not read as distinct materials. Foam should be duller and more damped, and rope should be creakier and more ropey. | The min and max extremes differ in kind. At minimum the bump is a tiny blip with almost no body. At maximum it is a long, ringing, low-frequency modal tone lasting about 0.5 s with strong horizontal lines. That reads as a resonant bell or drone rather than the same kind of impact. | The squeak is hard to see in the spectrograms. There is no clear rising or falling friction chirp at the defaults, so a key characteristic of fender rubbing is missing. | Seed variation is fine: the seeds share the same shape with different detail. The seeds also differ in peak level (0.373 vs 0.508), which a designer would have to normalise.
