  [ocean-anchor-drop] grader rejected: No clear anchor-splash event. The waveform builds gradually to a peak around 1.7 s, so it reads as a swelling rattle, not a drop followed by chain pay-out. A sound editor would expect a distinct entry impact or splash. | About 35% of the render is silent at defaults, and 56% at minimum settings. The sound is only about 2.5 s of content in a 3.5 s file, with a long dead tail. This is wasteful and sounds unfinished. | The size knob changes the sound very little. Small, medium and heavy have almost identical envelopes and spectrograms, with only a centroid shift. Heavy does not produce a convincing low-end thud or weight. | Chain rattle is visible as sparse, discrete, horizontal tonal streaks. These look like pitched ringing and not metallic clanks with a broadband transient, so the chain may sound like a tonal tinkle. | The tail toggle does almost nothing: its waveform and spectrogram are nearly the same as the default, so the knob is of little value. | Extremes are inconsistent. At minimum settings the sound is a short burst with a different shape and 56% silence. At maximum it is a long, swelling wash, which loses the 'drop then rattle' structure. These read as different sounds.
  [ocean-gangway-slam] grader rejected: Default steel slam reads as a low thud with a thin tail. The centroid is ~395 Hz and almost all energy sits below ~500 Hz, so it lacks the bright metallic clang or plate-impact transient the brief describes. | The default has 32% silence, meaning the waveform drops to near nothing for about the last 60% of its length. A metal plate slam should keep a decaying ring or rattle, so this reads as cut off or empty. | The wood option is not a metal gangway at all. It is a short, sparse tick with 51% silence and almost no sustain. It contradicts the brief, and it is a different sound from the other two materials. | At minimum range settings the sound is 65% silence with a weak, dull body (rms 0.038). At maximum it becomes a long, droopy 2 s ring, so the extremes behave like different kinds of sound. | Spectrograms show energy confined to the lowest band plus a few thin horizontal ring lines. There is little mid or high content for the impact, rattle or chain-and-plate jangle a sound editor expects. | Aluminium and the max-range version have long, smooth, almost pure decays, which sound like a sustained tonal hum rather than a slammed plate. Ring modes appear to be too few and too stable.
  [ocean-anchor-drop] grader rejected: The defaults do not read as an anchor drop. There is a roughly 0.6 s run-up of small blips, then one dense, near-continuous block of broadband noise with a flat-topped, square-ish envelope. A heavy chain rattling out should show discrete clanks with a speed-up, then a hard stop. This reads as a noise burst or rumble instead. | The splash and the chain rattle are not separable. The splash should be a distinct transient with a bright wash as the anchor hits the water, but there is no clear impact event. The low red band at the bottom reads as a steady drone and not a thud with a pitch drop. | The tail is mostly empty. About 45% of the render is near-silent, with a few sparse blips trailing after the main event, and the waveform fades into near-nothing. That is dead weight for a 3.2 s asset and feels like an abrupt, unnatural fall-off. | Range extremes change duration a lot (2.5 s to 4.0 s). At the maximum setting the chain events smear into a vertical-line comb pattern. At the minimum setting the sound is a short, boxy, low block (centroid 303 Hz) with no splash character, so it no longer reads as the same sound. | Seed variation is mostly timing and onset shift. Seed 3 starts the event at t=0, with no run-up, while the others have a lead-in, so the takes differ in structure instead of just detail. | The size knob mainly shifts the centroid and changes the block's width. It does not change the chain's character (link size, clank pitch), so 'heavy' sounds like a lower-passed version of the same sound.
